\chapter{The support chatbot that you attacked}\label{bot:chapter}

This chapter is the whole program of the support chatbot that the
lecture's attacks were aimed at: Mo, the assistant of Mossberry
Mercantile, a fictional web shop for Nordic home goods.  In three
rounds you tried to get a secret discount code out of Mo, to get
refunds you were not entitled to, and to plant instructions in product
reviews that Mo later summarised.  A switch on the lecturer's
dashboard replaced the vulnerable bot by a hardened one, in which each
mistake is fixed in code, and a meter showed what the room's attacks
cost, also when Mo refused.

You do not need to read the code to follow this chapter.  The program
is written as a \emph{literate program}: an explanation in ordinary
prose, with the code in numbered pieces, called chunks, placed where
the explanation needs them.  A chunk has a name in angle brackets, and
a chunk that mentions another chunk's name gets that chunk's code
inserted there; the computer gets the code put together in the right
order by a tool, and you get it in the order that makes sense to
read.  The prose says what each piece does and why, so you can skip
the code and still see how the bot works and where its mistakes are.
\Cref{bot:teaching} is the part to read if you read only one: it has
the four short files that the lecture showed on slides.  If you want
to attack the bot again on your own computer, \cref{bot:run} says how.

The program was written by an AI agent, Claude Opus~5.5, from a plan
that the lecturer approved.  Who checks that it does what this
chapter says?  The tests, the recorded attacks and the sandbox checks
described below.  Where a statement rests on reading a library's code
or running a command once instead, the text says so.

\section{Overview}\label{bot:overview}

The lecture's throughline is \enquote{who checks?}.  Each round of the
attack shows a check that a product left to the model, and the hardened
bot moves that check into code.  The bot therefore has two jobs: to be
vulnerable in exactly the intended ways, and to be safe to run on the
lecturer's laptop with a hundred phones attached.  Everything in this
document serves one of the two.

\Cref{bot:overview-figure} shows the parts.  Students' phones talk to the
\emph{phone server} on the local wifi.  The lecturer's \emph{dashboard}
is a second server that only the laptop itself can reach.  Both call
into one [[Bot]] object, which holds the rounds, the phones' sessions,
the limits and the meter.  The bot asks the model through the [[llm]]
library and runs the model's tool calls against the shop, which is
fake data in memory.  The whole process runs in a bubblewrap sandbox.

