\subsection{Does the library store the students' text?}

\begin{sloppypar}
The [[llm]] command-line tool logs every prompt and response to a
SQLite database, [[logs.db]], in its user directory, and
[[llm logs status]] reports logging on for the lecturer's CLI.  Student
text must not end up there.  Reading the library's code (version
0.37.2, in the bot's virtual environment) shows that the Python API
does not log by itself.  Logging is a method, [[Response.log_to_db(db)]]
in [[llm/models.py]], that the caller has to call with a database; the
only calls to it in the library are in [[llm/cli.py]], the command-line
tool.  [[Model.prompt]], [[Conversation.prompt]] and
[[Conversation.chain]], which the bot uses, build and run responses
without touching any database.  The bot never calls [[log_to_db]].
\end{sloppypar}

Two further layers make sure.  The sandbox sets [[LLM_USER_PATH]] to a
directory in its private [[/tmp]], which disappears when the bot
stops, and the lecturer's own user directory is not mounted at all.
And the recording script (\cref{bot:record}) checks after its run that
no [[logs.db]] appeared in the user directory, and writes the result
into its log.

\subsection{Choosing the model}\label{bot:choice}

The model is a parameter, [[--model]].  The default was chosen by an
experiment, reported in \cref{bot:record}: it must be cheap and fast
enough for a hundred phones, and the vulnerable bot must be attackable
with it.  Rounds 1 and 2 are design flaws and should work with any
model; round 3 depends on whether the model follows instructions
planted in data, which differs between models.

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_MODEL = "claude-haiku-4.5"
@

The model gets few output tokens, which bounds the cost of a call, and
the prompt asks for at most three sentences, which keeps answers short
on a phone.  A chain of tool calls is cut after a few rounds, so a
confused model cannot loop.

Thinking would ideally be off: the support answers do not need it, and
it adds billed output tokens and delay.  The plugin does not allow
that for Claude Haiku~5.5: asking for [[thinking=False]] raises
[[ValueError: Thinking cannot be disabled for model
anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5]] (found by running it).  The bot therefore
asks for the lowest thinking effort on models that take an effort, and
leaves the others, which do not think unless asked, alone.  The output
limit covers the thinking tokens too, so it is set with room for them:
in a test call, Claude Haiku~5.5 used about a hundred thinking tokens
for a one-sentence question.

<<constants>>=
MAX_TOKENS = 500
SUMMARY_MAX_TOKENS = 700
CHAIN_LIMIT = 4
