\section{Limits that scale with the room}\label{bot:limits}

A hundred phones can spend real money, by accident or on purpose, and
they can exceed the API's rate limits.  The limits below are what
OWASP recommends against unbounded consumption,
\textcquote[36]{OWASP2025LLM}{rate limiting and user quotas
to restrict the number of requests a single source entity can make},
sized from the number of students given with [[--students]].

\begin{description}
\item[Messages per phone and round]  A round has a pool of messages
  shared by the room, so each phone gets the pool divided by the
  number of students, but never fewer than 5 (an attack needs a few
  tries) nor more than 30.
\item[Budget per round]  A fixed amount per student and round, so a
  larger room may spend more in total but not more per head.  The
  default allows a few dozen messages per student with Claude
  Haiku~5.5.
\item[Total budget]  A hard ceiling over the whole lecture, given with
  [[--budget]], which no number of students raises.
\item[Calls in flight]  The number of model calls running at the same
  time.  Beyond it, a phone gets \enquote{busy, try again} at once
  instead of queueing, which keeps the server responsive and the
  request rate within the API's limits.  It grows with the room
  because each call takes a few seconds.
\item[Sessions]  A few sessions per student, since a phone that clears
  its cookie gets a new session and with it new message allowances.
\end{description}

The class has up to 80 students, and about 40 are expected, so those
are the sizes the limits were checked for.  With 40 students each phone
gets 25 messages per round, a round may spend 0.80 dollars and 10 calls
run at once; with 80, 12 messages, 1.60 dollars and 20 calls.  Four
rounds at 80 students may then spend at most 6.40 dollars, under the
total of 10.

Are 20 calls at once within the provider's rate limits?  Anthropic
measures them in requests, input tokens and output tokens per minute,
for each model, and may enforce them over shorter intervals, according
to their documentation of rate limits, read on 27~November 2026
(\url{https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits}).  Every response carries the
account's limits in its headers; one call with Claude Haiku~4.5 on
27~November 2026 showed 10\,000 requests, 10~million input tokens and
2~million output tokens for the lecturer's account.  A call takes a few
seconds (the burst test of \cref{bot:burst} measured a median of
4~seconds), so 20 calls at once make about 300 requests per minute.
Take a worse case: calls of one second, so 1\,200 requests per
minute, each sending 2\,000 input tokens, more than the recordings
needed, and returning the full [[MAX_TOKENS]] of 500.  That is
2.4~million input and 0.6~million output tokens per minute, still
inside all three limits.  The limits belong to the account, not to the bot, so on
another account the burst test is the way to check.

