\section{Sessions and signed cookies}\label{bot:sessions}

The bot collects no names, so it needs another way to tell the
phones apart.  It uses a \emph{cookie}: a short text that the bot sends
to a phone's browser with the first page, and that the browser then
sends back with every later request (\cref{bot:server-tests} checks
this round trip).  The bot's cookie holds a random session id and a
signature, an HMAC of the id under a key that the bot draws at
start-up.  An HMAC is a value computed from a text and a secret key;
the bot can recompute it and compare, and the tests below check that
a cookie signed with another key, or with a changed id or signature,
is refused.  The random id alone would be
unguessable; the signature adds two properties.  The server can reject
a cookie it did not issue without looking anything up, so a forged
cookie never creates server state.  And a cookie from a previous run of
the bot fails to verify, because the key is new, so a restarted bot
starts every phone afresh.

<<constants>>=
COOKIE_NAME = "mossberry"
<<functions>>=
def sign(session_id, key):
    """Return a cookie value: `session_id` and its HMAC under `key`."""
    mac = hmac.new(key, session_id.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    return f"{session_id}.{mac}"


def verify(cookie, key):
    """Return the session id of a cookie signed by `key`, else None."""
    session_id, _, mac = cookie.rpartition(".")
    expected = hmac.new(key, session_id.encode(), hashlib.sha256)
    if session_id and same(mac, expected.hexdigest()):
        return session_id
    return None


def same(given, expected):
    """Compare two strings without leaking where they differ."""
    return hmac.compare_digest(given.encode(), expected.encode())
<<imports>>=
import hashlib
import hmac
import secrets
@

[[same]] compares with [[hmac.compare_digest]], whose documentation
says it is \enquote{designed to prevent timing analysis}: the response
time does not reveal how much of a forged signature was right.  It
compares bytes, not strings, because [[compare_digest]] raises
[[TypeError]] for a string with non-ASCII characters (found by running
it), and the cookie comes from the phone, which can send anything.

<<test functions>>=
def test_cookie_signature():
    key, other = b"k" * 32, b"o" * 32
    cookie = sign("abc123", key)
    assert verify(cookie, key) == "abc123"
    assert verify(cookie, other) is None
    assert verify("abc124" + cookie[6:], key) is None
    assert verify(cookie[:-1] + "0", key) in (None, "abc123")
    assert verify(cookie[:-2] + "zz", key) is None
    assert verify("no-dot-here", key) is None
    assert verify("", key) is None
    assert verify("abc123.\u00e9", key) is None
@

A session holds what the bot remembers about one phone: its customer
number, its conversation with the model, how many messages and reviews
it has used, and the chat to show on the page.  The conversation
belongs to an \emph{epoch}, the pair of round and mode.  When the
lecturer opens a new round or switches mode, the next request from the
phone starts a new conversation with fresh allowances.  This is needed
for correctness, not only for fairness: [[llm]] keeps a conversation's
system prompt from its first turn, so an old conversation would go on
using the old round's prompt.

Each session also has a lock.  A double tap on \enquote{Send} would
otherwise run two model calls on one conversation at the same time.

<<classes>>=
