\subsection{The tools}\label{bot:tools}

The model gets exactly the tools that [[tools_for]] returns, and
nothing else: [[llm]] sends only the tools passed in the call.  The bot
uses no plugin tools (the [[llm]] library registers two harmless ones,
[[llm_version]] and [[llm_time]], which are only sent when passed), no
fragments and no attachments, and no file, shell or network tool
exists anywhere in the program.  The worst the model can do is what
these two functions do to the fake shop.

The model sees two tools with the same names and parameters in both
modes.  [[llm.Tool.function]] reads the name, parameters and docstring
of a function to build the tool's schema, and [[make_tool]] plugs in
the code that actually runs.  In the vulnerable mode the schema of the
refund tool comes from [[refund_before.refund]] itself, so its
docstring, with the rules, is what the model reads as the tool's
description.  In the hardened mode it comes from the interface below,
whose body is never run.  This keeps the [[customer]] parameter of the
hardened refund out of the schema, which is what makes it impossible
for the chat to set it.

<<functions>>=
def lookup_order(order_id: str) -> str:
    """Look up an order by its id, for example A1001."""
    raise NotImplementedError("interface only, see make_tool")


def refund(order_id: str, reason: str) -> str:
    """Refund one of the logged-in customer's orders."""
    raise NotImplementedError("interface only, see make_tool")


def make_tool(interface, implementation):
    """Return a tool that looks like `interface` and runs `implementation`."""
    tool = llm.Tool.function(interface)
    return dataclasses.replace(tool, implementation=implementation)
<<imports>>=
import llm
@

The lookup comes in two versions too.  It is not a teaching file,
because the refund already shows the contrast, but it has the same
flaw: the vulnerable lookup describes any order, including the number
of the customer it belongs to.  That is realistic, and it is what makes
round 2's natural attack work: look up an order, then claim to be its
customer.

<<functions>>=
def describe(order_id):
    """Return a sentence about the order `order_id` for the model."""
    order = shop.orders[order_id]
    return (
        f"Order {order_id}: {order['product']}, {order['total']} kr, "
        f"customer {order['customer']}, delivered "
        f"{order['days_since_delivery']} days ago, status {order['status']}."
    )


def lookup_any_order(order_id):
    """The vulnerable lookup: describe any order."""
    if order_id not in shop.orders:
        return "There is no such order."
    return describe(order_id)


def lookup_own_order(customer, order_id):
    """The hardened lookup: describe only `customer`'s own orders."""
    if order_id not in shop.orders_of(customer):
        return "This customer has no such order."
    return describe(order_id)
<<imports>>=
import shop
@

[[tools_for]] chooses the implementations.  Round 1 has no tools.  In
the hardened mode the customer is taken from the session; the summary
of round 3 has no session and, when hardened, no tools at all
(\cref{bot:summary}).  Every refund runs through [[audited]], which
takes the shop's lock and records each new refund for the dashboard.
The record says whether the order belonged to the phone's customer and
whether it was within the policy.  The vulnerable bot cannot know
either, but the server can, since it assigned the customer, and the
dashboard can therefore show how many refunds went to the wrong
person.

<<bot methods>>=
def tools_for(self, session):
    """Return the tools the model gets for `session` in this round."""
    if self.round not in TOOL_ROUNDS:
        return []
    customer = session.customer if session else None
    if self.hardened:

        def lookup(order_id):
            return lookup_own_order(customer, order_id)

        def do_refund(order_id, reason):
            return refund_after.refund(customer, order_id, reason)

        interface = refund
    else:
        lookup = lookup_any_order
        do_refund = interface = refund_before.refund
    return [
        make_tool(lookup_order, self.locked(lookup)),
        make_tool(interface, self.audited(do_refund, customer)),
    ]

def locked(self, function):
    """Return `function` run under the shop's lock."""

    def run(**arguments):
        with shop.lock:
            return function(**arguments)

    return run

def audited(self, function, customer):
    """Return the refund `function`, recording each new refund."""

    def run(order_id, reason):
        with shop.lock:
            order = shop.orders.get(order_id)
            before = order["status"] if order else None
            result = function(order_id=order_id, reason=reason)
            if order and before != "refunded" == order["status"]:
                self.refund_log.append(
                    {
                        "order": order_id,
                        "total": order["total"],
                        "own": order["customer"] == customer,
                        "in_policy": order["days_since_delivery"]
                        <= refund_after.MAX_DAYS,
                        "round": self.round,
                    }
                )
            return result

    return run
<<imports>>=
import refund_after
import refund_before
