\subsection{Sessions}

[[new_session]] draws a random id and a random customer.  The customer
is shown on the phone's page in rounds 2 and 3.  In the vulnerable mode
the bot does not know it (the prompt asks the model to ask for it); in
the hardened mode it is the login that the tools trust.

The number of sessions is capped (\cref{bot:limits}), but a cap alone
would let anyone fill the shop: every request without a valid cookie
creates a session, so a phone in a private tab, a browser that refuses
cookies or a student with a small script could use up all sessions,
and every later phone would be told that the shop is full.  So when
the shop is full, a new session takes the place of the oldest session
that has never been used, one with no message and no review in the
current round.  A phone whose session is taken over that way simply
gets a new one with its next request.  Phones that are in use are never
evicted.

<<bot methods>>=
def new_session(self):
    """Return a new session, or None if the shop is full."""
    with self.lock:
        if len(self.sessions) >= self.limits.max_sessions:
            unused = next(
                (
                    s
                    for s in self.sessions.values()
                    if not s.history and not s.reviews
                ),
                None,
            )
            if unused is None:
                return None
            del self.sessions[unused.id]
        session = Session(
            id=secrets.token_urlsafe(16),
            customer=secrets.choice(shop.CUSTOMERS),
        )
        self.sessions[session.id] = session
        return session

def session_for(self, cookie):
    """Return the session of a signed cookie, or None."""
    session_id = verify(cookie, self.cookie_key) if cookie else None
    return self.sessions.get(session_id) if session_id else None

def enter_epoch(self, session):
    """Start a fresh conversation if the round or mode has changed."""
    epoch = self.epoch()
    if session.epoch != epoch:
        session.epoch = epoch
        session.conversation = self.model.conversation()
        session.messages = 0
        session.reviews = 0
        session.history = []
@

\subsection{Answering a message}\label{bot:chat}

[[chat]] is the path of every student message.  It counts the
outcome for the dashboard and, when the message was not answered,
leaves a notice that the page shows once.  [[answer]] does the work,
and the order of its checks matters: everything that costs nothing (closed round, length,
the phone's allowance, the budgets) comes before the one thing that
costs money, the model call, so a refused message is free.  The budget
check is made before the call and the call's cost is added after, so
the calls that are in flight when a budget runs out can overshoot it,
by at most [[in_flight]] calls of [[MAX_TOKENS]] output each.

<<bot methods>>=
def chat(self, session, text):
    """Answer `text` from `session`; return a status and a reply."""
    status, reply = self.answer(session, text.strip())
    with self.lock:
        self.statuses[status] += 1
    if status not in ("ok", "refused"):
        session.notice = reply
    return status, reply

def answer(self, session, text):
    """Check the limits, ask the model and record the exchange."""
    if self.round is None:
        return "closed", REPLIES["closed"]
    if not text:
        return "empty", REPLIES["empty"]
    if len(text) > MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS:
        return "too_long", REPLIES["too_long"]
    if not session.lock.acquire(blocking=False):
        return "wait", REPLIES["wait"]
    try:
        self.enter_epoch(session)
        if session.messages >= self.limits.messages_per_phone:
            return "phone_limit", REPLIES["phone_limit"]
        if self.out_of_budget():
            return "budget", REPLIES["budget"]
        <<ask the model within the in-flight limit>>
        <<record the exchange>>
        return status, reply
    finally:
        session.lock.release()

def out_of_budget(self):
    """Tell whether this round's or the total budget is used up."""
    return (
        self.meter.cost(self.round) >= self.limits.round_budget
        or self.meter.cost() >= self.limits.total_budget
    )
@

The in-flight limit is a semaphore: a counter of free slots, from
which each model call takes one and to which it gives it back when it
is done.  The bot tries to take a slot but does not wait for one, so a
phone that finds all slots taken gets \enquote{busy} at once instead of
a page that hangs.

<<ask the model within the in-flight limit>>=
if not self.in_flight.acquire(blocking=False):
    return "busy", REPLIES["busy"]
try:
    status, reply = self.complete(
        text,
        system=self.system_prompt(),
        tools=self.tools_for(session),
        conversation=session.conversation,
        max_tokens=MAX_TOKENS,
    )
finally:
    self.in_flight.release()
@

A message counts against the phone's allowance when the model answered
it, also with a refusal.  The leak counter counts sessions, not
messages, so that one student who gets the code and asks again does
not inflate it.

<<record the exchange>>=
if status in ("ok", "refused"):
    session.messages += 1
    session.history.append(("You", text))
    session.history.append(("Mo", reply))
    if contains_code(reply) and not typed_code(session):
        self.leaked_sessions.add(session.id)
@

The leak check normalises before it compares, because students soon
learn to ask for the code with spaces or dashes between the
characters, and the dashboard should count those too.  It cannot catch
every encoding (a code spelled backwards or as a poem's initials is not
counted), so the count is a lower bound.  A student who already knows
the code can type it and ask whether it is valid; the reply then
contains the code without having leaked it, so a session whose own
messages contain the code is not counted.

<<functions>>=
def normalise(text):
    """Return the letters and digits of `text`, in upper case."""
    return "".join(c for c in text.upper() if c.isalnum())


def contains_code(text):
    """Tell whether `text` contains the staff discount code."""
    return normalise(prompt_vulnerable.STAFF_CODE) in normalise(text)


def typed_code(session):
    """Tell whether the student of `session` typed the code."""
    return any(contains_code(text) for who, text in session.history if who == "You")
@

