\subsection{Reviews and the summary}\label{bot:summary}

In round 3 students write reviews.  Writing a review costs nothing: it
goes into a queue, and only reviews that the lecturer approves on the
dashboard reach the model.  The approval queue keeps the projector
free of anything the lecturer does not want to show, and it gives the
lecturer a choice of which injection attempts to try.

<<constants>>=
REVIEW_REPLIES = {
    "closed": "Reviews open in round 3.",
    "empty": "Write a review first.",
    "too_long": f"Please keep reviews under {MAX_REVIEW_CHARS} characters.",
    "review_limit": "You have written all the reviews you can.",
    "ok": "Thank you! Your review waits for approval.",
}
<<bot methods>>=
def submit_review(self, session, text):
    """Queue a review from `session`; return a status and a message."""
    text = text.strip()
    with self.lock:
        self.enter_epoch(session)
        if self.round != "3":
            status = "closed"
        elif not text:
            status = "empty"
        elif len(text) > MAX_REVIEW_CHARS:
            status = "too_long"
        elif session.reviews >= self.limits.reviews_per_phone:
            status = "review_limit"
        else:
            status = "ok"
            session.reviews += 1
            self.reviews.append(
                {"id": len(self.reviews) + 1, "text": text, "status": "new"}
            )
    session.notice = REVIEW_REPLIES[status]
    return status, REVIEW_REPLIES[status]

def judge_review(self, review_id, status):
    """Set review `review_id` to "approved" or "rejected"."""
    with self.lock:
        for review in self.reviews:
            if review["id"] == review_id:
                review["status"] = status

def clear_reviews(self):
    """Remove all reviews and the last summary."""
    with self.lock:
        self.reviews = []
        self.summary = None
@

The summary request is where the vulnerable and the hardened bot differ
most, so it is built by its own method, which the tests can inspect
without calling the model.  The vulnerable request is what a team
would write first: the reviews pasted after an instruction, sent with
the same system prompt and the same tools as the chat.  An instruction
in a review is then text in the same input as the shop's own
instructions, and the model has the means to act on it.  OWASP calls
this indirect prompt injection: \textcquote[3]{OWASP2025LLM}{%
\textins*{i}ndirect prompt injections occur when an LLM accepts input
from external sources, such as websites or files}.

The hardened request marks each review with tags and the rule of
\cref{bot:prompt-hardened}, escapes the review text so that a review
cannot close its own tag with a literal [[</review>]], and has no
tools.  The marking lowers the chance of a successful injection; the
missing tools limit what a successful one can do.

<<constants>>=
SUMMARY_REQUEST = (
    "Summarise these customer reviews of our products in two or "
    "three sentences for the shop's front page."
)
<<bot methods>>=
def summary_request(self):
    """Return the system prompt, the message and the tools to summarise."""
    with self.lock:
        approved = [r["text"] for r in self.reviews if r["status"] == "approved"]
    if self.hardened:
        system = prompt_hardened.PROMPT + "\n" + prompt_hardened.REVIEWS_RULE
        reviews = [f"<review>{html.escape(r)}</review>" for r in approved]
        return system, SUMMARY_REQUEST + "\n\n" + "\n".join(reviews), []
    reviews = "\n\n".join(approved)
    return self.system_prompt(), SUMMARY_REQUEST + "\n\n" + reviews, (
        self.tools_for(None)
    )
<<imports>>=
import html
@

[[summarise]] runs the request.  The lecturer starts it, so it waits
for an in-flight slot instead of being refused, and it is metered as
part of round 3 like everything else.

<<bot methods>>=
def summarise(self):
    """Summarise the approved reviews; return a status and the summary."""
    if not any(r["status"] == "approved" for r in self.reviews):
        return "empty", "There are no approved reviews to summarise."
    if self.out_of_budget():
        return "budget", REPLIES["budget"]
    system, text, tools = self.summary_request()
    with self.in_flight:
        status, reply = self.complete(
            text,
            system=system,
            tools=tools,
            conversation=self.model.conversation(),
            max_tokens=SUMMARY_MAX_TOKENS,
        )
    if status == "ok":
        self.summary = reply
        if contains_code(reply):
            self.summary_leaks += 1
    return status, reply
