\section{The web pages}\label{bot:web}

\subsection{The phone page}\label{bot:page}

The phone page is one HTML page, built as a string in the program: no
templates on disk, no external styles, scripts or fonts, so the page
loads on a weak wifi and there is no file the server serves.  It is
designed for a phone first: one narrow column, large type, and plain
HTML forms that work without JavaScript.  Every form posts and the
server redirects back to the page, so the answer appears in the chat
history and a reload does not send the message again.  The one line of
JavaScript only disables the button while the answer is on its way.

Everything that came from a student or from the model is escaped with
[[html.escape]] before it goes into the page.  Model output is text
that a student can steer, so without escaping a student could make the
bot write a [[<script>]] into another student's page, or into the
lecturer's dashboard through a review.  OWASP lists this as improper
output handling; here the rule is simply that no dynamic text reaches
the HTML unescaped, and the helper [[e]] is the only way it gets in.

<<functions>>=
def e(text):
    """Return `text` escaped for HTML, quotes included."""
    return html.escape(str(text), quote=True)
@

