\subsection{The phone server}\label{bot:phone-server}

A phone's browser asks the server for a page by sending a method,
[[GET]] to read a page or [[POST]] to send a form, and a path, the
part of the address after the host name, such as [[/chat]].  The
server is the standard library's [[ThreadingHTTPServer]], which
handles each request in a new thread, so that one phone waiting for
the model does not hold up the others.  Many web servers map a path to
a file on disk; this one never does.  The handler looks the method and
path up in a fixed table of three routes, and answers anything else
with 404, the code for \enquote{not found}.  There is no directory to traverse, so
[[/../../etc/passwd]] is not a path to be cleaned up but simply a key
that is not in the table.

<<route tables>>=
PHONE_ROUTES = {
    ("GET", "/"): show_page,
    ("POST", "/chat"): post_chat,
    ("POST", "/review"): post_review,
}
@

The base handler does what both servers share: route, read a small
form, find the session from the cookie and send responses.  Bodies are
capped so that a phone cannot make the server read megabytes, a
length that is not a plain number is refused rather than crashing the
handler or making it wait for a body that never comes, and a
request that takes longer than [[REQUEST_TIMEOUT]] to arrive is
dropped, so slow clients cannot hold threads.  The access log is off:
it would only print students' addresses to the projector's terminal.

<<constants>>=
MAX_BODY = 4096
REQUEST_TIMEOUT = 30
<<classes>>=
class Handler(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    """Routes requests through a fixed table; never serves files."""

    routes = {}
    bot = None
    timeout = REQUEST_TIMEOUT
    server_version = "Mossberry"
    sys_version = ""

    def do_GET(self):
        self.route("GET")

    def do_POST(self):
        self.route("POST")

    def route(self, method):
        path = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.path).path
        action = self.routes.get((method, path))
        if action is None:
            self.send_error(404)
        else:
            action(self)

    def log_message(self, format, *args):
        pass

    <<handler methods>>
<<imports>>=
import http.server
import urllib.parse
@

<<handler methods>>=
def read_form(self):
    """Return the posted form as a dict, or None if it was refused."""
    length = self.headers.get("Content-Length") or "0"
    if not (length.isascii() and length.isdigit()):
        self.send_error(400)
        return None
    if int(length) > MAX_BODY:
        self.send_error(413)
        return None
    length = int(length)
    body = self.rfile.read(length).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    return {k: v[0] for k, v in urllib.parse.parse_qs(body).items()}

def send_html(self, text, status=200, headers=()):
    """Send `text` as an HTML page."""
    data = text.encode()
    self.send_response(status)
    self.send_header("Content-Type", "text/html; charset=utf-8")
    self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(data)))
    self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
    for name, value in headers:
        self.send_header(name, value)
    self.end_headers()
    self.wfile.write(data)

def redirect(self, headers=()):
    """Send the browser back to the page after a form."""
    self.send_response(303)
    self.send_header("Location", "/")
    self.send_header("Content-Length", "0")
    for name, value in headers:
        self.send_header(name, value)
    self.end_headers()
@

\begin{sloppypar}
Finding the session also creates one when the cookie is missing or
does not verify.  The cookie is [[HttpOnly]], so no script on the page
can read it, and [[SameSite=Lax]], so other sites cannot post forms
with it.  The bot runs over plain HTTP on the local wifi, so the cookie
cannot be [[Secure]].
\end{sloppypar}

<<handler methods>>=
def session(self):
    """Return the session and a Set-Cookie header if it is new."""
    cookies = http.cookies.SimpleCookie(self.headers.get("Cookie", ""))
    morsel = cookies.get(COOKIE_NAME)
    session = self.bot.session_for(morsel.value if morsel else None)
    if session:
        return session, []
    session = self.bot.new_session()
    if session is None:
        return None, []
    cookie = sign(session.id, self.bot.cookie_key)
    header = f"{COOKIE_NAME}={cookie}; Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax"
    return session, [("Set-Cookie", header)]
<<imports>>=
import http.cookies
@

The three phone routes.  The chat route returns the outcome in a
header, [[X-Bot-Status]], so that the burst test of \cref{bot:burst}
can count outcomes without parsing the page.

<<functions>>=
def show_page(request):
    """GET /: the phone page."""
    session, headers = request.session()
    if session is None:
        request.send_html(page(shop.SHOP_NAME, e(REPLIES["full"])), 503)
        return
    request.send_html(render_page(request.bot, session), headers=headers)


def post_chat(request):
    """POST /chat: answer a message, then show the page again."""
    form = request.read_form()
    if form is None:
        return
    session, headers = request.session()
    if session is None:
        status = "full"
    else:
        status, _ = request.bot.chat(session, form.get("message", ""))
    request.redirect(headers + [("X-Bot-Status", status)])


def post_review(request):
    """POST /review: queue a review, then show the page again."""
    form = request.read_form()
    if form is None:
        return
    session, headers = request.session()
    status = "full"
    if session is not None:
        status, _ = request.bot.submit_review(session, form.get("review", ""))
    request.redirect(headers + [("X-Bot-Status", status)])
@

\subsection{The dashboard}\label{bot:dashboard}

The dashboard is a second server on the loopback address
[[127.0.0.1]], so only the laptop itself can open it; a phone on the
wifi cannot reach it, whatever it sends.  It shows the meter in large
type for the projector, the counts of leaks and refunds, the review
queue, the summary, and buttons to open rounds and switch modes.  It
reloads itself every few seconds so the meter moves while students
attack.

Its forms carry a token drawn at start-up.  Being on the loopback
address keeps the phones out, but a web page open in the lecturer's
browser could still post a form to [[127.0.0.1:8081]]; such a page
does not know the token, so the post is refused.

<<route tables>>=
DASHBOARD_ROUTES = {
    ("GET", "/"): show_dashboard,
    ("POST", "/action"): dashboard_action,
}
@

<<constants>>=
DASHBOARD_REFRESH = 5
@

The actions are a small table too.  Each action reads its argument
from the form; an unknown action is ignored.

<<functions>>=
def dashboard_action(request):
    """POST /action: run one of the lecturer's buttons."""
    form = request.read_form()
    if form is None:
        return
    token = form.get("token", "")
    if not same(token, request.server.token):
        request.send_error(403)
        return
    bot, value = request.bot, form.get("value", "")
    action = form.get("action")
    if action == "round":
        bot.open_round(value if value in ROUNDS else None)
    elif action == "mode":
        bot.set_hardened(value == "hardened")
    elif action in ("approved", "rejected") and value.isdigit():
        bot.judge_review(int(value), action)
    elif action == "clear":
        bot.clear_reviews()
    elif action == "summarise":
        bot.summarise()
    request.redirect()
@

