    form = {"Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"}
    exact = "message=" + "x" * (MAX_BODY - len("message="))
    assert request(phone, "POST", "/chat", exact, form).status == 303
    cookie = request(phone, "GET", "/").getheader("Set-Cookie")
    assert "HttpOnly" in cookie and "SameSite=Lax" in cookie
@

\section{Starting the bot}\label{bot:main}

[[make_servers]] builds the two servers.  The dashboard's address is
fixed to the loopback address here, not taken from the command line,
so no option can expose it to the wifi.  Each server gets a handler
class of its own with its route table and the bot.

<<functions>>=
def make_servers(bot, host, port, dashboard_port):
    """Return the phone server and the dashboard server for `bot`."""

    class PhoneHandler(Handler):
        routes = PHONE_ROUTES

    class DashboardHandler(Handler):
        routes = DASHBOARD_ROUTES

    PhoneHandler.bot = DashboardHandler.bot = bot
    phone = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer((host, port), PhoneHandler)
    dashboard = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer(
        ("127.0.0.1", dashboard_port), DashboardHandler
    )
    phone.daemon_threads = dashboard.daemon_threads = True
    dashboard.token = secrets.token_urlsafe(16)
    return phone, dashboard
@

The phones need the laptop's address on the wifi.  Connecting a UDP
socket sends no packet but makes the operating system choose the
outgoing interface, whose address is the one the phones can reach.  The
address used, from the documentation range TEST-NET-1, is never
contacted.

<<functions>>=
def lan_address():
    """Return this machine's address on its default network."""
    with socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_DGRAM) as udp:
        try:
            udp.connect(("192.0.2.1", 9))
            return udp.getsockname()[0]
        except OSError:
            return "127.0.0.1"
<<imports>>=
import socket
@

The guess can be wrong: a laptop on a VPN, or behind a network that
forwards a public address, should give the address itself with
[[--public-url]].  Either way the address ends up in a line of LaTeX
written by the launcher (\cref{bot:sandbox}), where a character such as
[[%]] or [[}]] would break the slides' build, so [[phone_url]] accepts
only the characters that a plain web address needs.

<<constants>>=
SAFE_URL = re.compile(r"https?://[A-Za-z0-9.:/_-]+")
<<functions>>=
def phone_url(args):
    """Return the address that the phones should open."""
    url = args.public_url or f"http://{lan_address()}:{args.port}/"
    if not SAFE_URL.fullmatch(url):
        raise SystemExit(f"Not a plain web address: {url!r}")
    return url
