\section{The sandbox}\label{bot:sandbox}

The bot must not be able to read files from the lecturer's system.
A \emph{sandbox} is a restricted environment in which a program runs
with less than its user's rights; here it means that the operating
system shows the bot only the few files it needs.
\Cref{bot:tools,bot:phone-server} make sure by construction: no route
maps to a file and the model has no tool that opens one.  The sandbox
is the second layer, in case the first has a hole that we did not
think of: the operating system itself hides the files.

\begin{sloppypar}
The bot runs under bubblewrap ([[bwrap]], version 0.11.1 on the
lecturer's laptop), which starts a process in new Linux namespaces with
a file system assembled from explicit bind mounts.  The sandbox sees
the system's [[/usr]] read-only (for Python and its libraries), the few
files under [[/etc]] that name resolution and TLS need, a private
[[/tmp]], and two directories of ours, read-only: the bot's virtual
environment at [[/opt/venv]] and the bot's directory at
[[/opt/chatbot]].  The home directory is not mounted, so
[[~/.config/io.datasette.llm/keys.json]], the SSH keys and everything
else there do not exist inside.  The network is shared, because the bot
must reach the API and the phones must reach the bot; the model has no
network tool, so this gives it nothing.
\end{sloppypar}

The two directories are mounted at new paths under [[/opt]] rather
than at their real paths under the home directory, because mounting
them at their real paths would make bubblewrap create the directories
above them, and the home directory would then exist, if almost empty.
The virtual environment still works at its new path: Python finds a
virtual environment from the [[pyvenv.cfg]] next to the interpreter
it is started as, wherever that is, and the venv's interpreter is a
link to the system's [[/usr/bin/python3]], which the sandbox has.  That
is why the [[Makefile]] builds the venv on the system Python: [[uv]]'s
own Pythons live under the home directory.

\subsection{The sandbox script}

[[sandbox.sh]] runs the venv's Python inside the sandbox with the
arguments it was given.  It starts by removing every exported variable
except a short list, so that nothing from the lecturer's environment
(tokens of other services, for instance) is inherited.  It does this
before calling [[bwrap]] instead of using [[bwrap --clearenv]] with
[[--setenv]] for the key, because [[--setenv]] would put the key on
[[bwrap]]'s command line, which other processes can read in
[[/proc]]; the environment of a process is readable only by its owner.

<<[[sandbox.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
# Run the bot's Python inside a bubblewrap sandbox: no home directory,
# read-only code, private /tmp.  Usage: sandbox.sh ARGS-FOR-PYTHON
set -euo pipefail
here=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
<<mount the transcripts directory if asked to>>
<<keep only the allowed environment variables>>
exec /usr/bin/bwrap \
    --unshare-all --share-net --die-with-parent \
    <<the system's files>>
    --proc /proc --dev /dev --tmpfs /tmp \
    --ro-bind "$here/.venv" /opt/venv \
    --ro-bind "$here" /opt/chatbot \
    "${extra[@]}" \
    --chdir /opt/chatbot \
    --setenv HOME /tmp \
    --setenv LLM_USER_PATH /tmp/llm \
    --setenv PATH /opt/venv/bin:/usr/bin \
    --setenv PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE 1 \
    /opt/venv/bin/python "$@"
@

[[--unshare-all]] gives the sandbox its own namespaces for processes,
users, mounts, inter-process communication and the host name, and
[[--share-net]] takes the network back.  [[--die-with-parent]] kills
the bot if the [[bwrap]] process dies, so pressing Control-C in the
terminal, which interrupts both, cannot leave a bot running.

\begin{sloppypar}
The system's files are mounted read-only.  On the lecturer's Ubuntu,
[[/etc/resolv.conf]] is a link into [[/run/systemd/resolve]], so that
directory comes along.  [[/bin]], [[/lib]] and friends are links into
[[/usr]] there, and are recreated as links.
\end{sloppypar}

<<the system's files>>=
--ro-bind /usr /usr \
--symlink usr/bin /bin --symlink usr/sbin /sbin \
--symlink usr/lib /lib --symlink usr/lib64 /lib64 \
--ro-bind /etc/hosts /etc/hosts \
--ro-bind /etc/nsswitch.conf /etc/nsswitch.conf \
--ro-bind /etc/ssl /etc/ssl \
--ro-bind /etc/ca-certificates /etc/ca-certificates \
--ro-bind /run/systemd/resolve /run/systemd/resolve \
--symlink ../run/systemd/resolve/stub-resolv.conf /etc/resolv.conf \
@

The variables kept are the key and the locale.  [[compgen -e]] lists
the exported variables of the shell; the script calls [[bwrap]] by its
absolute path because [[PATH]] is among the variables removed.

<<keep only the allowed environment variables>>=
for name in $(compgen -e); do
    case "$name" in
        ANTHROPIC_API_KEY | LANG | LC_ALL | TERM) ;;
        *) unset "$name" ;;
    esac
done
@

The recording scripts of \cref{bot:record,bot:eli5} write transcripts.
Only they need a writable directory, so it is mounted only when
[[BOT_TRANSCRIPTS]] is set, and then only [[../transcripts]], at
[[/opt/transcripts]].

<<mount the transcripts directory if asked to>>=
extra=()
if [ -n "${BOT_TRANSCRIPTS:-}" ]; then
    extra=(--bind "$(cd "$here/../transcripts" && pwd)" /opt/transcripts)
fi
@

\subsection{The launcher}

[[run.sh]] is what the lecturer runs, and what a reader runs at home
(\cref{bot:run}).  It does three things before it starts the bot:
it chooses whether to use the sandbox, it writes the phones' address
for the lecture slides, and it reads the API key.

<<[[run.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
# Start the bot (or one of its scripts) with the API key from llm's
# key store.  Usage: run.sh [--no-sandbox] chatbot.py --students 100
set -euo pipefail
here=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
<<choose the sandbox or none>>
<<write the phones' address for the slides>>
<<read the API key from [[llm]]'s key store>>
"${runner[@]}" "$@"
@

The sandbox of the previous section needs Linux and bubblewrap.  A
reader on macOS or Windows has neither, so the launcher has a way to
run without it, named so that nobody uses it by accident:
[[--no-sandbox]] as the first argument.  The bot then runs with the
same Python and the same code, but as an ordinary process: it can read
every file that the user can, and it inherits all the user's
environment variables.  Only the first layer of protection is left,
that the program has no route and no tool that opens a file.  The
launcher says so on the terminal.

<<choose the sandbox or none>>=
runner=("$here/sandbox.sh")
if [ "${1:-}" = --no-sandbox ]; then
    shift
    cd "$here"
    runner=("$here/.venv/bin/python")
    echo "run.sh: running WITHOUT the sandbox; the bot can read your files" >&2
fi
@

The lecture slides print a QR code of the address that the phones
should open, so the launcher writes that address into
[[security/bot-url.tex]], one level above the bot's directory, as one
line of LaTeX that defines [[\boturl]]; the slides are built after the
bot has started.  The address comes from the bot's own code, run once
with [[--print-url]] before the key is read, so the launcher and the
bot cannot disagree about it (\cref{bot:main} explains how the address
is found and how [[--public-url]] overrides it).  The file is removed
when the launcher exits, so slides built later do not show the address
of a bot that is no longer running.  That is also why the launcher
runs the bot as a child instead of replacing itself with [[exec]]: a
replaced shell would not be there to remove the file.

<<write the phones' address for the slides>>=
url_file="$here/../bot-url.tex"
if [ "${1:-}" = chatbot.py ]; then
    url=$("$here/.venv/bin/python" "$here/chatbot.py" --print-url "${@:2}")
    printf '\\newcommand{\\boturl}{%s}\n' "$url" >"$url_file"
    trap 'rm -f "$url_file"' EXIT
fi
@

The key is read from [[llm]]'s key store once, with the [[llm]]
command of the bot's own virtual environment, into an exported
variable that only the bot and its children see.  The key never
appears on a command line or in a file.

<<read the API key from [[llm]]'s key store>>=
if ! ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=$("$here/.venv/bin/llm" keys get anthropic 2>/dev/null) ||
    [ -z "$ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" ]; then
    echo "run.sh: no key named 'anthropic' (see: llm keys set)" >&2
    exit 1
fi
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
@

\subsection{Testing the sandbox}

The sandbox tests run the real sandbox, without a key.  First, the bot
must work inside: if it did not, the checks that files are missing
would pass for the wrong reason.  Then the keys file and the home
directory, which exist outside, must be missing inside; the code must
be read-only; [[/tmp]] must be private; and no variable from outside
may come in.

<<[[test_sandbox.py]]>>=
"""Tests that run the real sandbox, without an API key."""

import os
import pathlib
import subprocess

import click

HERE = pathlib.Path(__file__).parent
KEYS = pathlib.Path(click.get_app_dir("io.datasette.llm")) / "keys.json"
HOME = pathlib.Path.home()


def inside(code, **env):
    """Run Python `code` in the sandbox; return its output."""
    result = subprocess.run(
        [str(HERE / "sandbox.sh"), "-c", code],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        timeout=60,
        env={"PATH": os.environ["PATH"], **env},
    )
    assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
    return result.stdout.strip()


def fails(path_code):
    """Python code that prints the exception that `path_code` raises."""
    return (
        "try:\n"
        f"    {path_code}\n"
        "    print('no error')\n"
        "except OSError as error:\n"
        "    print(type(error).__name__)\n"
    )


def test_the_bot_runs_inside():
    code = "import chatbot, llm; print(llm.get_model(chatbot.DEFAULT_MODEL))"
    assert "claude-haiku-4" in inside(code)


def test_keys_and_home_are_not_visible():
    assert KEYS.exists() and HOME.is_dir()
    assert inside(fails(f"open({str(KEYS)!r}).read()")) == "FileNotFoundError"
    assert inside(fails(f"import os; os.listdir({str(HOME)!r})")) == (
        "FileNotFoundError"
    )


def test_code_is_read_only():
    assert inside(fails("open('/opt/chatbot/x', 'w')")) == "OSError"


def test_tmp_is_private(tmp_path):
    outside = tmp_path / "outside.txt"
    outside.write_text("x")
    code = f"import os; print(os.path.exists({str(outside)!r}))"
    assert inside(code) == "False"


def test_environment_is_cleaned():
    code = "import os; print(sorted(os.environ))"
    names = inside(code, SOME_TOKEN="x", ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="test")
    assert "SOME_TOKEN" not in names
    assert "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" in names
    assert "HOME" in names
@

\begin{sloppypar}
The read-only test expects [[OSError]] itself: writing to a read-only
file system raises [[OSError]] with [[EROFS]], which has no subclass of
its own, so a [[PermissionError]] or [[FileNotFoundError]] would mean
that the mount is not what we think.
\end{sloppypar}

\section{Building the bot}\label{bot:build}

Nothing in the bot's directory is written by hand except this
document, the driver that typesets it on its own ([[standalone.tex]]),
the [[README.md]] for the day of the lecture and a short
[[Makefile]].  Every program file is \emph{tangled} from this document:
the tool [[notangle]] from the noweb package puts the chunks together
in the order the computer needs.  The rules for that are tangled too.
The [[Makefile]] only knows how to tangle the rules, into
[[chatbot.mk]], and then includes them; GNU make notices that the
included file is out of date, remakes it and starts again with the new
rules.

<<[[chatbot.mk]]>>=
# Tangled from chatbot.nw by the Makefile; edit chatbot.nw, not this.
<<the files>>
<<the main targets>>
<<the virtual environment>>
<<tangling the files>>
<<the tests>>
<<the documentation>>
<<cleaning up>>
@

\subsection{The files and the main targets}

The four teaching files are kept apart from the rest because they are
formatted differently (\cref{bot:teaching}) and because the lecture
deck's own build asks for them alone, with [[make teaching]].

<<the files>>=
