[[make bot]] is all that running the bot needs: the virtual environment
and the code.  It needs Python, [[uv]] and noweb, but no LaTeX, so a
reader who only wants to run the bot (\cref{bot:run}) can skip the
typesetting.  [[make]] alone also builds this document.

<<the main targets>>=
.PHONY: all bot teaching
all: bot chatbot.tex chatbot.pdf
bot: venv ${CODE}
teaching: ${TEACHING}
@

\subsection{The virtual environment}

The bot has its own virtual environment with exactly the versions it
was tested with.  It is built on the system's Python, not one that
[[uv]] downloads, because [[uv]] keeps its Pythons under the home
directory, which the sandbox does not mount (\cref{bot:sandbox}).
[[PYTHON]] can be set on the command line for a machine whose Python
is elsewhere, for a run without the sandbox.

<<the virtual environment>>=
VENV=		.venv
PYTHON?=	/usr/bin/python3
PACKAGES=	llm==0.37.2 llm-anthropic==0.31 anthropic==1.13.0 \
		pytest black

.PHONY: venv
venv: ${VENV}/.installed
${VENV}/.installed:
	uv venv --python ${PYTHON} ${VENV}
	uv pip install --python ${VENV}/bin/python ${PACKAGES}
	touch $@
@

\subsection{Tangling}

The teaching files are formatted by [[black]] at the slides' width of
69 characters, so that what the deck shows is exactly what [[black]]
would leave unchanged; the other Python files at [[black]]'s usual
width.  [[cpif]] replaces a file only when its content changed, so an
edit to one chunk does not make every file look new.  The tangling
waits for the virtual environment, since [[black]] lives there.

<<tangling the files>>=
${TEACHING}: chatbot.nw | venv
	notangle -R"[[$@]]" $< | cpif $@
	${VENV}/bin/black -q --line-length 69 $@

${MODULES} ${TESTS}: chatbot.nw | venv
	notangle -R"[[$@]]" $< | cpif $@
	${VENV}/bin/black -q $@

${SCRIPTS}: chatbot.nw
	notangle -R"[[$@]]" $< | cpif $@
	chmod +x $@
@

\subsection{The tests}

[[make test]] runs every test that needs no model: the offline tests
next to the code and the sandbox tests of \cref{bot:sandbox}.  On a
machine without bubblewrap, [[make test-offline]] runs only the first.

<<the tests>>=
.PHONY: test test-offline
test: bot
	${VENV}/bin/python -m pytest -q test_chatbot.py test_sandbox.py
test-offline: bot
	${VENV}/bin/python -m pytest -q test_chatbot.py
