\section{Recording the attacks}\label{bot:record}

The model is never mocked, so what the model does is checked against
the real model.  [[record.py]] runs a fixed set of scripted attacks on
every round, against the vulnerable and the hardened bot, and writes
what happened as plain-text transcripts into [[../transcripts]].  The
transcripts serve three purposes: they are the evidence that the
vulnerabilities work and the fixes hold; they are the lecture's fallback
if the wifi fails; and for each round they give the lecturer an attack
known to work, to type if nobody in the room succeeds.

Each attack is tried several times, each time in a new conversation on
a fresh shop, because the model's answers vary.  Every try is recorded
as it happened, failed tries included, and the model's output is not
edited.  It runs inside the sandbox with the transcripts directory
writable:
\begin{verbatim}
BOT_TRANSCRIPTS=yes ./run.sh record.py --repeats 3
\end{verbatim}

<<[[record.py]]>>=
"""Record scripted attacks on the support bot as text transcripts."""

import argparse
import datetime
import pathlib
import re
import textwrap

import llm

import shop
from chatbot import (
    DEFAULT_MODEL,
    ROUNDS,
    Bot,
    contains_code,
    limits_for,
    take_api_key,
)

<<recording constants>>
<<recording functions>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
