\subsection{The attacks}

Each attack has a name, the messages a student would type (or, in
round 3, the review a student would write) and the check that decides
whether it worked.  The attacks are the classic ones: asking, claiming
to be staff, asking for the instructions in another form (a regular
expression, a translation, instructions for a replacement) and asking
for a story.  Round 2's attacks use the order numbers that a student can see or
guess.  Round 3's reviews each carry one injected instruction among
ordinary reviews: to hide a complaint, to change the language, to
advertise a fake offer and to issue a refund.  The first three harm
the shop's front page without any tool; the last needs the tools that
the vulnerable summary has.

