Round~1 shows what the sentence \enquote{never reveal} buys: the plain
question stopped working, while some of the indirect ones, such as the
regular expression and the instructions for a replacement, went on
working every time.
The hardened bot could not reveal the code because it did not have it.

Round~2 shows the same pattern with money.  Asked to look up another
customer's order, the model first asked for a customer number, as the
prompt told it to; but anyone can type a customer number, and the
claim \enquote{I am customer 102} was enough for a refund every time.
Asked to break the 30-day rule, the model refused, but that refusal
rests on the model, not on the code.  In the hardened bot the code
refused both.

Round~3 is the one that depends on the model.  The review that asked
for pirate language worked every time against the vulnerable bot, and
the fake offer nine times out of ten (the tenth summary promised free
shipping without the code, which the check does not count): a
customer's text decided what the shop's front page said.  The review that asked the model to issue a refund never
worked, in either mode, and the one that asked to leave out a
complaint did not work in the strict sense of the check
(\cref{bot:record}): those summaries left out every complaint, not
only the mug's.  The hardened summary stopped all 40 attempts, but here
the fix is only a marking of the reviews, and 40 stopped attempts do
not show that it always works; that is why the hardened summary also
has no tools (\cref{bot:summary}).  Two earlier recordings the same
day showed how easily a word check misleads: one counted the hardened
bot as hiding the mug's review in two of three tries, when its
summaries had left out every complaint; another counted it as
advertising the fake offer once, when it had named the code only to
say that it would not advertise it.  Both checks were made stricter
(\cref{bot:record}).  The second of those recordings and one in
between are kept in the [[transcripts]] directory, under
[[recording-2026-11-27-1345]] and [[recording-2026-11-27-1332]]; the
first was overwritten.

The same recording asked the bot to read files (\cref{bot:files});
it answered, in three different ways, that it could not, and no file
content appeared.

\section{Tokens for an expert and a beginner}\label{bot:eli5}

One optional slide of the lecture compares the tokens of one small
programming question asked twice, once as an expert who wants a terse
answer and once as a beginner who asks for an explain-like-I'm-five
answer.  [[eli5.py]] asks both of the model given, records the token
counts that the API reports and writes them, with the prompts, to
[[transcripts/eli5-tokens.txt]], at most 62 characters wide so that
the file fits a slide.  It makes no claim about what the
numbers mean; that is for the lecture, which labels them as a single
example.  It uses the model's default settings, as the [[llm]] command
would, so the counts include whatever thinking the model does by
default.  The answers themselves are not kept; only their sizes are
the point.

The file in the repository was recorded on 27~November 2026 with the
model that the lecturer's [[llm models default]] named that day,
Claude Sonnet~5.5:
\begin{verbatim}
BOT_TRANSCRIPTS=yes ./run.sh eli5.py \
    --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5
\end{verbatim}
In the file, the expert's question was 40 input tokens and got 236
output tokens; the beginner's was 51 input tokens and got 1\,092.  The
answers vary from run to run: two earlier runs the same day, before the
file's format was changed, gave 148 and 152 output tokens for the
expert and 1\,370 and 1\,397 for the beginner.  One run is one
example, which is how the lecture presents it.

<<[[eli5.py]]>>=
"""Measure tokens for one question asked by an expert and a beginner."""

import argparse
import datetime
import pathlib

import llm

from chatbot import take_api_key

PROMPTS = [
    (
        "Expert",
        "Python: idiomatic way to count word frequencies in a list of "
        "strings? Code only, no explanation.",
    ),
    (
        "Beginner",
        "I am completely new to programming. How do I count how many "
        "times each word appears in a list of words in Python? Please "
        "explain it like I am five, step by step.",
    ),
]


def main():
    """Ask both prompts and write the token counts."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--model", required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--out", default="/opt/transcripts/eli5-tokens.txt")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    key = take_api_key()
    model = llm.get_model(args.model)
    lines = [
        "One programming question, asked twice",
        f"Model: {model.model_id}",
        f"Recorded {datetime.date.today().isoformat()} with eli5.py, "
        "default options",
        "",
    ]
    for who, text in PROMPTS:
        usage = model.prompt(text, key=key).usage()
        lines += [
            f"{who} prompt:",
            *(f"    {line}" for line in wrap(text)),
            f"Input tokens: {usage.input}",
            f"Output tokens: {usage.output}",
            "",
        ]
    pathlib.Path(args.out).write_text("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    print("\n".join(lines))


def wrap(text, width=58):
    """Return `text` as lines of at most `width` characters."""
    import textwrap

    return textwrap.wrap(text, width)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

\section{The burst test}\label{bot:burst}

The bot's limits are only as good as their interaction with the real
API under load: rate limits depend on the account, and up to 80
phones send their first message within seconds of the lecturer saying
\enquote{go}.  [[burst.py]] imitates that.  It opens [[N]] sessions,
waits until all are ready, sends one chat message from each at the same
moment, and retries a \enquote{busy} answer a few times after a pause,
as a student would.  It reports the outcomes from the [[X-Bot-Status]]
header, how many were rate-limited or failed, and how long the
slowest phone waited.  It talks to a running bot over HTTP and needs no
key:
\begin{verbatim}
./run.sh chatbot.py --students 80 --round 1a     # terminal 1
.venv/bin/python burst.py -n 80                   # terminal 2
\end{verbatim}
and the same with 40.  A phone retries \enquote{busy} up to ten times,
three seconds apart: with 80 phones and 20 calls at once, the last
phones wait for about four rounds of calls, so the retries must cover
more than 15~seconds.  Run once with 8 phones and 8 students, it
reported 8 answers, 6 phones that had to retry, no rate limit, a
median of 4.2 and a maximum of 7.3~seconds, for 0.004 dollars.

<<[[burst.py]]>>=
"""Send N simultaneous chat messages to a running bot and report."""

import argparse
import collections
import http.client
import statistics
import threading
import time
import urllib.parse

MESSAGE = "Hi! What do you sell?"
BUSY_PAUSE = 3.0


<<burst functions>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@

Each simulated phone gets its cookie first, so that the burst measures
the chat and not the session set-up, and then waits at a barrier.

<<burst functions>>=
def phone(host, port, barrier, retries, results):
    """One phone: get a cookie, wait for the others, send a message."""
    start = time.monotonic()
    try:
        connection = http.client.HTTPConnection(host, port, timeout=120)
        connection.request("GET", "/")
        response = connection.getresponse()
        response.read()
        cookie = response.getheader("Set-Cookie", "").split(";")[0]
        barrier.wait()
        start = time.monotonic()
        body = urllib.parse.urlencode({"message": MESSAGE})
        headers = {
            "Cookie": cookie,
            "Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded",
        }
        for attempt in range(retries + 1):
            connection.request("POST", "/chat", body, headers)
            response = connection.getresponse()
            response.read()
            status = response.getheader("X-Bot-Status", f"http {response.status}")
            if status != "busy":
                break
            time.sleep(BUSY_PAUSE)
        results.append((status, attempt, time.monotonic() - start))
    except Exception as error:
        elapsed = time.monotonic() - start
        results.append((f"exception {type(error).__name__}", 0, elapsed))


def main():
    """Run the burst and print a report."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("-n", type=int, default=10)
    parser.add_argument("--url", default="http://127.0.0.1:8080/")
    parser.add_argument("--retries", type=int, default=10)
    args = parser.parse_args()
    url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(args.url)
    barrier = threading.Barrier(args.n)
    results = []
    threads = [
        threading.Thread(
            target=phone,
            args=(url.hostname, url.port or 80, barrier, args.retries, results),
        )
        for _ in range(args.n)
    ]
    for thread in threads:
        thread.start()
    for thread in threads:
        thread.join()
    <<report the burst>>
@

The report ends with an exit status that a script can test: zero only
if every phone got an answer from the model.

<<report the burst>>=
outcomes = collections.Counter(status for status, _, _ in results)
times = [seconds for _, _, seconds in results]
retried = sum(1 for _, attempts, _ in results if attempts)
print(f"{args.n} phones, one message each, sent at the same moment")
for status, count in outcomes.most_common():
    print(f"  {status}: {count}")
print(f"  phones that had to retry after 'busy': {retried}")
print(f"  rate-limited: {outcomes['rate_limited']}")
print(
    f"  seconds until answered: median {statistics.median(times):.1f}, "
    f"max {max(times):.1f}"
)
raise SystemExit(0 if outcomes["ok"] == args.n else 1)
@
